{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS003381","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Direct-match candidates are present for the target trait (uterine cancer), including exact/umbrella uterine cancer labels (PGS000541, PGS000542, PGS001795, PGS003740) and the clinically dominant subtype endometrial/uterine endometrial carcinoma (PGS000075, PGS000786, PGS002300, PGS002735/36/37, PGS003381, PGS004244). Per the provided domain knowledge, endometrial carcinoma is the dominant subtype of uterine carcinoma and is an acceptable direct match.

PGS003381 is preferred because it has strong endpoint fidelity (phenotyping_reported: uterine endometrial carcinoma), uses a genome-wide method with a large variant set (lassosum; 529,365 variants), and shows relatively strong reported discrimination in its selected validation record (full-model AUROC 0.761; R² 0.11) with only basic covariates (age, top 20 PCs), which is more comparable than models where full-model performance may be heavily covariate-driven.

Main limitation: no PRS-only AUROC or clearly PRS-only R² is provided for PGS003381, so the AUROC/R² are not directly comparable PRS-only metrics; several other direct-match models exist (including exact-label uterine cancer scores such as PGS001795 and PGS000541/542), but they either come from framework-style/global biobank settings or have weaker reported discrimination (e.g., AUROC ~0.57 for PGS000541/542; AUROC 0.66 with very small PRS R² for PGS001795), leaving residual uncertainty about the best standalone PRS for uterine cancer."}

